#---
# purpose: "atom-keyed map iteration uses the same name order as the language term order"
#---
#
# The allocation counts are part of the ordering contract. Comparing atom
# names must not materialize fz strings. Native construction allocates the one
# 64-byte source map; normalization reuses that destination without allocating.
# That allocation is classified as Map, not Other; total allocation stays fixed.
#
# - native: map `1/64`, list cons `15/240`, struct `3/96`, scalar box `7/112`;
# - interpreter: map `3/144`, list cons `15/240`, struct `3/96`, scalar box
#   `10/160`.
#
# Both have Other `0/0`; native total is `26/512`, interpreter total `31/640`.
# Dynamic list heads use the same source-retention guard as statically known heads.
# Each pair is `allocs/bytes`. The fixed atom-list input to
# `Enum.sort/1` is deliberate: feeding it `Map.keys/1` would change merge-sort's
# work-list shape when the bug is fixed and would confound comparator cost with
# a different input order.
def main() do
  map = %{z: 1, a: 2, m: 3}

  dbg(Kernel.compare(:z, :a))
  dbg(Enum.sort([:z, :a, :m]))
  dbg(Map.keys(map))
  dbg(Map.values(map))
  dbg(Map.to_list(map))

  stats = Process.heap_alloc_stats()
  dbg({
    stats[:map_allocs],
    stats[:map_bytes],
    stats[:list_cons_allocs],
    stats[:list_cons_bytes],
    stats[:struct_allocs],
    stats[:struct_bytes],
    stats[:scalar_box_allocs],
    stats[:scalar_box_bytes],
    stats[:other_allocs],
    stats[:other_bytes],
    stats[:allocs],
    stats[:bytes]
  })
end
